{
  "sticker": "umbrella",
  "called": "the beach umbrella",
  "skeleton": "a beach umbrella standing upright: root at the pole's tip in the sand; pole on it; canopy on a bone at the top of the pole (the open canopy, a turned canopy with its frill fluttering, and the closed canopy for opening, which slides along the pole).",
  "sheets": [
    {
      "id": "parts",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "prompt": "Image 1 is a sticker of a beach umbrella. Draw it as a cut-out paper puppet: its separate pieces laid out on one sheet, the same drawing style, colours, texture, line and proportions as in image 1, every piece at the size it has in image 1 (if they do not all fit, draw them all a little smaller, at the same scale as each other).\n\nDraw each piece on its own, spread out over the sheet with wide empty gaps between them, no piece touching or overlapping another:\n1. the open canopy alone, as in image 1: its red-and-white striped top, the knob on top and its scalloped frilled edge, seen as in image 1, without the pole.\n2. the same open canopy turned round a little on its pole so that its red and white panels have swapped places, its frill fluttering: the scallops lifted and rippling in a breeze. Without the pole.\n3. the wooden pole alone: from just under the canopy, with its collar and the two thin struts that reach up from the collar, down to its rounded tip.\n4. the canopy closed: furled tight into a slim red-and-white bundle with its frill tucked in and its knob on top, as a beach umbrella is before it opens, without the pole.\n\nEvery piece is drawn whole, including what is normally hidden behind another piece, with its edges clean and smooth. It is an object, not a character: no face, no eyes, no mouth. No text, no labels, no numbers, no outline around the pieces beyond the drawing's own line, no shadows, no water, no sand, no splashes or drops. Fully transparent background."
    }
  ],
